#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091980 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ed5facf-f654-5890-974f-bbdcd9641515" :knowledge-point/title "Applying De Moivre's Theorem to Fractions"}] :question/id #uuid "7e804bd6-9ddf-58cb-8013-0c0623ecdd07" :question/math-academy-id "e-5270" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac{1}{\\left[\\cos (\\theta) +\\textrm{i}\\sin(\\theta) \\right]^2}$." :question/worked-solution "First, note that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\left[ \\cos(\\theta) +\\textrm{i}\\sin(\\theta) \\right]^2} = \\left[ \\cos(\\theta) +\\textrm{i}\\sin(\\theta) \\right]^{-2}\n$$\n\nDe Moivre's Theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\left[ \\cos(\\varphi) + \\textrm{i}\\sin(\\varphi) \\right]^n = \\cos(n\\varphi) + \\textrm{i}\\sin{(n\\varphi)}\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=-2$ and\n\n$$\n\\varphi=\\theta\n$$\n\nSubstituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1)/([\\cos (θ) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (θ)]^{2}) &= [\\cos (θ) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (θ)]^{-2} \\\\\n&= \\cos (-2θ) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (-2θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\cos(-\\varphi) = \\cos (\\varphi)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\sin(-\\varphi) = -\\sin(\\varphi)\n$$\n\nfor any input $\\varphi$, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos(-2\\theta) + \\textrm{i}\\sin(-2\\theta) = \\cos(2\\theta) - \\textrm{i}\\sin{(2\\theta)}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091981 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5055bd50-a6aa-547d-9ce3-573d79ac2e91" :knowledge-point/title "Applying De Moivre's Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "cbd9cb3f-bb2e-5505-8f53-068e77717edb" :question/math-academy-id "e-5271" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\left[\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{\\theta}{10} \\right) + \\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\theta}{10}\\right) \\right]^4$." :question/worked-solution "De Moivre's Theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\left[ \\cos(\\varphi) + \\textrm{i}\\sin(\\varphi) \\right]^n = \\cos(n\\varphi) + \\textrm{i}\\sin{(n\\varphi)}\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=4$ and\n\n$$\n\\varphi=\\dfrac{\\theta}{10}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[\\cos (\\frac{θ}{10}) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (\\frac{θ}{10})]^{4} &= \\cos (4 \\cdot \\frac{θ}{10}) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (4 \\cdot \\frac{θ}{10}) \\\\\n&= \\cos (\\frac{2θ}{5}) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (\\frac{2θ}{5})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091987 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbc91582-8d44-5de1-9c98-1c5de173eefb" :knowledge-point/title "Applying De Moivre's Theorem With a Negative Sine"}] :question/id #uuid "ea0c744c-8814-5a78-8059-23346051ef1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5291" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac{1}{\\left[\\cos (2\\theta) -\\textrm{i}\\sin (2\\theta) \\right]^4}$." :question/worked-solution "First, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1)/([\\cos (2θ) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (2θ)]^{4}) &= [\\cos (2θ) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (2θ)]^{-4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor expressions involving subtraction, De Moivre's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\left[ \\cos (\\varphi) - \\textrm{i}\\sin(\\varphi) \\right]^n = \\cos(n\\varphi) - \\textrm{i}\\sin{(n\\varphi)}\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=-4$ and\n\n$$\n\\varphi=2\\theta\n$$\n\nSubstituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[\\cos (2θ) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (2θ)]^{-4} &= \\cos (-4 \\cdot 2θ) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (-4 \\cdot 2θ) \\\\\n&= \\cos (-8θ) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (-8θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\cos(-\\varphi) = \\cos (\\varphi)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\sin(-\\varphi) = -\\sin(\\varphi)\n$$\n\nfor any input $\\varphi$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos (-8θ) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (-8θ) &= \\cos (8θ) - [- \\textrm{i}\\sin (8θ)] \\\\\n&= \\cos (8θ) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (8θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
